Let G = (V, E) be an undirected graph (a protein–protein interaction network), where V is a set of proteins and E ⊆ V × V is the set of experimentally observed pairwise interactions. Suppose V is partitioned into a subset M ⊆ V of membrane proteins and its complement V \ M of non-membrane proteins, where membership in M is determined by independent biological annotation (e.g., predicted transmembrane domains or subcellular localization). Define the membrane subinteractome as the induced subgraph G[M] = (M, E ∩ (M × M)). A membrane protein complex is a subset C ⊆ M whose elements form a stable physical assembly; the goal is to recover such complexes as densely connected subgraphs of G[M], given that only a very small reference set R of membrane complexes with fully characterized internal interactions is available for training or validation.

Develop a computational/graph-theoretic procedure that:

(i) extracts from G a reliable approximation Ĝ_M of the true membrane subinteractome, taking into account that direct membership annotation of M may be noisy and that interactions in E may have varying confidence weights w : E → [0, 1];

(ii) given Ĝ_M, partitions or covers M by candidate complexes {C_1, …, C_k} (possibly overlapping) that maximize internal connectivity/cohesion while remaining consistent with the small reference set R;

(iii) provides quantitative performance guarantees or evaluation criteria (precision, recall, or matching scores against R) in the regime where |R| ≪ k.

Formulate, analyze, and justify such a procedure, addressing in particular how the scarcity of characterized membrane complexes (|R| small) can be overcome — for example via transfer of structural patterns from the soluble subinteractome G[V \ M], via semi-supervised or unsupervised graph clustering on Ĝ_M, or via incorporation of auxiliary biological features as edge/vertex weights.

